// What the finalizer buys, as an exact structural property rather than a
// statistical one.
//
// `Map` selects buckets with `hash & (cap - 1)`, so only the LOW bits matter.
// Bit 0 of an FNV-1a result is bit 0 of its starting value XOR the parity of
// the input bytes' low bits. Every key below is built from letters with an
// even ASCII code, so that parity is 0 for all of them and bit 0 of an
// unfinalized hash is the same for every key -- which puts every key in a
// bucket whose index has that same bit 0, reaching HALF the buckets and no
// more. splitmix64's finalizer mixes high bits down into low ones, which is
// the axis a starting value cannot reach, and the halving disappears.
//
// Measured against 60 random seeds, with keys built exactly as below:
// unfinalized reached 32 of 64 buckets under every seed and its largest
// bucket ran 66 to 76; finalized reached 64 of 64 under every seed with a
// largest bucket of 40 to 53. A later sweep over 300 seeds widened the
// finalized largest bucket to 40-56 and left the unfinalized range at 66-76.
// So `reached` is a structural assertion, not a threshold, and the bound on
// the largest bucket has room on both sides.
//
// EACH ASSERTION THIS FIXTURE PRINTS CARRIES A RISK, AND EACH RATE IS DERIVED
// BELOW. Stating one rate beside a silent sibling invites the reader to read
// the silence as zero, which is the defect this paragraph exists to close.
// Bucket counts are not independent -- they sum to 1999 -- so these are
// union-bound figures, which agree with the independent-product form to four
// significant figures at these numbers.
//
//   * `reached 64 of 64` FLAKES if chance leaves a bucket empty. That is
//     (63/64)^1999 per bucket over 64 buckets: about 1 in 7 * 10^11 per run.
//   * `largest < 64` FLAKES if chance overfills one bucket. A bucket's count is
//     Binomial(1999, 1/64) with mean 31.23, so the rate is that distribution's
//     right tail from 64 up, over 64 buckets: about 1 in 110 000 per run.
//   * `largest < 64` LETS A MUTATION ESCAPE if the unfinalized hash passes it.
//     The unfinalized hash reaches 32 buckets, so its counts are
//     Binomial(1999, 1/32) with mean 62.47, and the escape is the probability
//     that all 32 stay under 64: about 1 in 1.1 * 10^8 per run.
//
// The bound read 60 before, whose flake rate is about 1 in 6100 -- eighteen
// times worse -- against an escape rate of about 1 in 1.9 * 10^14. Moving to 64
// buys that eighteen-fold flake reduction and pays for it with an escape that is
// still about a thousand times rarer than the flake remaining at 64, and about
// 18 000 times rarer than the flake it removes. 64 sits 8 above the widest
// measured finalized maximum (56, over 300 seeds) and 2 below the lowest
// measured unfinalized largest bucket (66).
//
// DO NOT RAISE IT PAST 66. At 66 the escape rate is about 1 in 660 000 against
// a flake rate of about 1 in 520 000 -- the two have met -- and above 66 the
// seed-only design section 2 of the spec rejected starts passing outright, at
// which point this fixture stops distinguishing the shipped hash from the
// rejected one, which is the whole reason it exists.
fn key_at(i: Int) -> String {
    let alpha = ["b", "d", "f", "h", "j", "l", "n", "p", "r", "t", "v", "x"]
    let mut n = i
    let mut out = ""
    let mut d = 0
    while d < 6 {
        out = "${out}${alpha[n % 12]}"
        n = n / 12
        d = d + 1
    }
    out
}

fn main() {
    let cap = 64
    let n = 1999
    let mut counts = [0; cap]
    let mut i = 0
    while i < n {
        let slot = key_at(i).hash() & (cap - 1)
        counts[slot] = counts[slot] + 1
        i = i + 1
    }
    let mut reached = 0
    let mut largest = 0
    let mut j = 0
    while j < cap {
        if counts[j] > 0 { reached = reached + 1 }
        if counts[j] > largest { largest = counts[j] }
        j = j + 1
    }
    println("keys ${n} into ${cap} buckets")
    println("reached ${reached} of ${cap}")
    println("largest under 64 ${largest < 64}")
}
